# Release owners. Combined with the `protected-branches` ruleset's "Require review from Code Owners"
# (default branch and release/**), a PR that touches any of these paths needs an approving review from one of
# them. Changing `version` in gradle.properties drives the automatic tag and release, and the other
# paths decide what runs, with the release credentials, when it does.
#
# There is intentionally no catch-all `*` rule: all other paths stay unowned and do NOT require these
# reviewers. CODEOWNERS is last-match-wins, so keep the patterns below non-overlapping.
#
# For now the code owner is @satran004 alone. The release-ops standard names the org team
# @bloxbean/release-owners, which stays the reviewer of the `release` and `github-pages` environments; switch
# every line below back to the team when JOUST has more maintainers. A code owner can't approve their own pull
# request, so while there is a single owner, their own PRs to these paths merge through the `protected-branches`
# ruleset's organization-admin bypass (pull_request mode).

# This file, so the protection can't be removed without a release owner.
/.github/CODEOWNERS      @satran004

# Every workflow runs with the repository's secrets once it is on a protected branch.
/.github/workflows/      @satran004
/.github/scripts/        @satran004

# Version bump -> automatic tag and release.
/gradle.properties       @satran004

# Build logic evaluated by the credentialed release job: publication scope, signing, nmcp, the dependency
# versions it resolves (gradle/libs.versions.toml), and the Gradle distribution that runs it.
/build.gradle            @satran004
/settings.gradle         @satran004
/gradle/libs.versions.toml @satran004
/gradle/wrapper/         @satran004
/gradlew                 @satran004
/gradlew.bat             @satran004
